#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489359} :activity/id #uuid "c50fc124-2e70-50d8-bf4f-018c42fcb65d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6c9d7c-6f8b-549f-ade8-d1e4ebbf0134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266377} {:answer/id #uuid "2db15dbd-e51f-532f-9bfd-157b9f779945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266548} {:answer/id #uuid "3c660db5-3c31-5643-8113-78583f356468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266674} {:answer/id #uuid "a5eecdbb-ca77-56e2-9dcd-5a6810654073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267646} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a5f3a4-eed9-50d5-8e07-a411ebbe09d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266377} :answer-field/id #uuid "50bb18b4-57b1-5ebe-9423-935bc3b0a3cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9379a450-3ef7-54fa-b6cb-c35ea0f78eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-133382" :question/problem "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{yz}\\cdot (\\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{yz}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot ({e}^{yz}+yz{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{yz}(1+yz)}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}}{(1+1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =-\\frac{{e}^{1\\cdot 0}(1+1\\cdot 0)}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "769313f6-43dc-5919-9553-b36396031990"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002afc6b-3ae6-5e4d-9e87-6404be2be05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479355} {:answer/id #uuid "a95258ee-6056-5703-bec4-79d09d5e79de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479610} {:answer/id #uuid "d3510268-8c4c-529e-8b03-2fd8c635e27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479679} {:answer/id #uuid "dd42a3d6-7453-56cc-b6fd-7e6f716d0a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479686} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc862f5-359e-590e-930c-858b88ff0363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479686} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff4a0306-bebb-5a0b-88e8-580bcd8a3741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c2a62d8-6d93-5394-bb23-b474c6edf899" :question/math-academy-id "q-65128" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y+1$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y+1$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} :step/id #uuid "aae4262b-4042-5b50-9965-2172062f55a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f60ae37-1165-5440-bde6-f01cffce801b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266987} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8382b2-cf1c-5bb5-b534-beacbde2bdfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267414} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b8eef4-ff75-5267-860e-a762c49305b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267662} {:answer/id #uuid "c4658edd-5348-55cc-a2aa-5ce494911f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267898} {:answer/id #uuid "f3239213-7c2d-5989-a3bf-a19a7229c158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267414} :answer-field/id #uuid "a30a187b-9301-5273-a095-e7add20e9085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33686134-3f7d-5a05-9109-0d2ac79b776f" :question/math-academy-id "q-133373" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})=6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=3\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b1968108-fe35-55fa-b259-cedf76219668" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489329}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b45857f-4c74-59d9-8e35-85de5f5c9034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488645} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0ab324-a63f-56ba-b911-46caeedd3017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488721} {:answer/id #uuid "88ad4c18-6273-5c23-9f0f-fff73fe14cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488740} {:answer/id #uuid "a2acdb8d-7243-568f-a9db-fdae13525746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488792} {:answer/id #uuid "aed29a6c-7da5-5f41-b70d-1d66d427908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488740} :answer-field/id #uuid "df0c4030-39f2-5bc9-a0e8-ac2b199792a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c68f1c1a-c6a6-5448-b77c-02d88ae0390d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36482" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{x}^{2}-4y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-4x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3-5x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=1$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-2{y}^{3}{e}^{{y}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{x}^{2}-4y)={x}^{2}-4 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-4x)={x}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-5x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-2{y}^{3}{e}^{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."} :step/id #uuid "be2117cb-2320-5d47-9072-a89c15136337" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489384}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489384 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6926f5e-dc21-5da5-b35d-b718df8a66ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477365} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f6a95f-1511-52d2-946b-1d648665d214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477365} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6da62ee-a826-5879-91f1-9c7966d71193" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4cb90f6-70fc-53f0-9088-772c160341cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477367} {:answer/id #uuid "26573f3b-930d-57c0-9106-c38022948389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477368} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b11426-2cab-5199-bc57-42df399c747e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477653} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9478de04-8fb1-57f2-973d-3770f1978c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477369} {:answer/id #uuid "a7bf79dc-14ad-5ba5-85de-39e95f01562c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477370} :answer-field/id #uuid "766dd905-2ba1-5ac0-b3f1-1f815b83a4fe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3caf1655-4ce4-5a5f-995b-a06e33069b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65127" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{3}y-x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-y$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2x$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-3$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x)={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y)={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}-2y)={e}^{x+y}-2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}-2x)={e}^{x+y}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x-1})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "fdc1b998-1ffd-53d7-9edb-119354e86ad0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489350}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488462}]}
